No.  The nethack license is a modified GPL (That's not 100% accurate,
but I don't care, for the purpose of this discussion).  You are not in
breach of the license if you charge a fee for your distribution, so
long as you meet all the other terms of the license, which mostly
requires attribution and requires that you make the source code
available.

The license specifically states that you may charge for your
distribution, either directly for the means of transfer, or as
consideration for a warranty.   Downstream from you, the recipient
must have the same rights that were granted to you when you received
it.  This is actually the whole point of licenses like this.   The
parts of your work that are not derivative work -- and the diligence
is your responsibility -- may be licensed differently and distributed
separately.  That approach gets complicated, believe me.  (Ask me some
time about the closed-source RFID module in the open-source Inventory
Control system).

There's not even a mechanism for a license like this to stop you from
asking for consideration (e.g. money or barter) for your distribution,
whether it's a derivative work or not.

Want a real life example?   Look no further than the $2500.00 Red Hat
Enterprise Linux, which one could argue is a $2500.00 copy of nethack
with a bunch of other stuff bundled.


